      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2011 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "Cardiovascular magnetic resonance (CMR) has a recognized role in diagnosing and monitoring coronary artery disease (CAD). Multiple studies have shown that CMR can predict adverse outcomes. We reviewed contemporary available literature to establish the role of CMR with late gadolinium enhancement (LGE) in predicting mortality and major adverse cardiac events (MACEs) in patients with CAD. Meta-analysis of available prospective studies showed that the presence of LGE increases the hazards of death by more than 4 times and of MACE by almost 4 times. The size of LGE (per gram or percent) increases the hazards of death and MACE by 4% and 5%, respectively. The presence and size of LGE predict mortality and MACE in CAD. Various parameters derived from LGE images enhance the predictive value. Large randomized controlled trials are needed to establish the actual value of LGE and other derived parameters in the wider population."
      },
